// A Unix-shaped shell, over a filesystem that lives in this machine's memory
// and nowhere else.
//
// It replaces `hold()`: the kernel used to end by spinning so that a
// screenshot had something to photograph, and now it ends by asking. Nothing
// under it is an operating system -- there is no process, no file descriptor
// and no disk -- so "Unix-shaped" means the shapes a person recognises (a `$`
// prompt, `..`, absolute and relative paths) rather than any of the mechanism
// behind them.
//
// Everything it prints goes through `con_line`, which writes the framebuffer
// console and the serial port at once, because a test reads the serial port
// and a person reads the screen and they must not drift.

// The screen is blanked first because `con_demo` has just scrolled forty lines
// of test text past, and a shell that starts halfway down someone else's output
// looks like it crashed.
sh_run() {
  fs_init();
  con_blank();
  sh_hi();
} return void;

// The banner is a separate function only because `sh_run` has three actions in
// it already and the loop has to be called from somewhere.
sh_hi() {
  con_line("id shell -- an in-memory filesystem, and no libc under it");
  con_line("type help for the commands");
  sh_loop();
} return void;

// Never returns. The counter is a `word` and never changes, for the reason
// `hold` records: an `int` incremented here reaches two billion in about a
// second, wraps negative and leaves the loop, and the machine powers off in
// the middle of someone typing.
sh_loop() {
  word spins = 0;
  while(spins >= 0) {
    sh_once();
  }
} return void;
